WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:01.006 --> 00:01.696 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

00:03.716 --> 00:04.996 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:05.356 --> 00:06.566 align:middle
Thanks for joining us today.

00:08.036 --> 00:10.986 align:middle
I would like to begin by
acknowledging the tragic loss

00:10.986 --> 00:12.016 align:middle
and tremendous hardship

00:12.016 --> 00:14.156 align:middle
that people are experiencing
both here

00:14.156 --> 00:15.716 align:middle
in the United States
and around the world.

00:17.026 --> 00:18.816 align:middle
The coronavirus outbreak
is first

00:18.816 --> 00:20.596 align:middle
and foremost a public
health crisis,

00:20.826 --> 00:22.976 align:middle
and the most important
response is coming from those

00:22.976 --> 00:25.276 align:middle
in the front lines in hospitals,

00:25.276 --> 00:27.456 align:middle
emergency services,
and care facilities.

00:28.036 --> 00:29.516 align:middle
On behalf of the
Federal Reserve,

00:29.516 --> 00:31.626 align:middle
let me express our
sincere gratitude

00:32.076 --> 00:34.826 align:middle
to those dedicated individuals
who put themselves at risk,

00:34.986 --> 00:38.496 align:middle
day after day, in service
to others and to our nation.

00:39.096 --> 00:42.736 align:middle
The forceful measures that
we as a country are taking

00:42.736 --> 00:45.256 align:middle
to control the spread of
the virus have brought much

00:45.256 --> 00:47.016 align:middle
of the economy to
an abrupt halt.

00:48.016 --> 00:50.216 align:middle
Many businesses have closed,
people have been asked

00:50.216 --> 00:53.376 align:middle
to stay home, and basic
social interactions are

00:53.376 --> 00:54.296 align:middle
greatly curtailed.

00:55.276 --> 00:57.786 align:middle
People are putting their
lives and livelihoods on hold,

00:58.396 --> 01:00.796 align:middle
at significant economic
and personal cost.

01:01.806 --> 01:05.286 align:middle
All of us are affected, but the
burdens are falling most heavily

01:05.776 --> 01:07.656 align:middle
on those least able
to carry them.

01:08.826 --> 01:11.006 align:middle
It is worth remembering that
the measures we are taking

01:11.006 --> 01:14.616 align:middle
to contain the virus represent
an investment in our individual

01:14.676 --> 01:15.586 align:middle
and collective health.

01:16.786 --> 01:19.236 align:middle
As a society, we should
do everything we can

01:19.456 --> 01:21.286 align:middle
to provide relief to those

01:21.286 --> 01:22.846 align:middle
who are suffering
for the public good.

01:25.046 --> 01:27.926 align:middle
While many standard economic
statistics have yet to catch

01:27.926 --> 01:29.676 align:middle
up with the reality
we're experiencing,

01:30.066 --> 01:33.266 align:middle
it's clear that the effects
on the economy are severe.

01:33.966 --> 01:35.966 align:middle
Millions of workers
are losing their jobs:

01:36.376 --> 01:38.716 align:middle
Next week's jobs report
is expected to show

01:38.716 --> 01:39.756 align:middle
that the unemployment rate,

01:40.246 --> 01:42.846 align:middle
which was at 50-year
lows just two months ago,

01:43.136 --> 01:44.786 align:middle
has surged into double digits.

01:45.546 --> 01:48.196 align:middle
Household spending has
plummeted as people stay home,

01:48.706 --> 01:51.026 align:middle
and measures of consumer
sentiment have

01:51.086 --> 01:52.096 align:middle
fallen precipitously.

01:52.836 --> 01:56.076 align:middle
Hotels, airlines,
restaurants, department stores,

01:56.076 --> 01:58.236 align:middle
and other retailers have
been particularly hard hit.

01:59.826 --> 02:02.536 align:middle
Manufacturing output fell
sharply in March and is likely

02:02.536 --> 02:04.386 align:middle
to drop even more
rapidly this month,

02:04.856 --> 02:07.096 align:middle
as many factories have
temporarily closed.

02:07.826 --> 02:10.246 align:middle
Overall, economic
activity will likely drop

02:10.246 --> 02:12.526 align:middle
at an unprecedented rate
in the second quarter.

02:13.916 --> 02:17.166 align:middle
Inflation is also being held
down, reflecting weaker demand

02:17.166 --> 02:19.606 align:middle
as well as significantly
lower energy prices.

02:20.406 --> 02:22.116 align:middle
Both the depth and the duration

02:22.116 --> 02:25.016 align:middle
of the economic downturn are
extraordinarily uncertain

02:25.526 --> 02:27.046 align:middle
and will depend in large part

02:27.046 --> 02:29.616 align:middle
on how quickly the virus
is brought under control.

02:30.486 --> 02:32.506 align:middle
The severity of the
downturn will also depend

02:32.506 --> 02:35.246 align:middle
on the policy actions taken
at all levels of government

02:35.626 --> 02:37.886 align:middle
to cushion the blow and
to support the recovery

02:38.286 --> 02:40.096 align:middle
when the public health
crisis passes.

02:40.616 --> 02:44.756 align:middle
The Federal Reserve's response
is guided by our mandate

02:44.756 --> 02:46.986 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

02:46.986 --> 02:49.516 align:middle
for the American people, along
with our responsibilities

02:49.516 --> 02:51.636 align:middle
to promote the stability
of the financial system.

02:52.416 --> 02:55.246 align:middle
We are also committed to
using our full range of tools

02:55.246 --> 02:57.436 align:middle
to support the economy
in this challenging time.

02:58.926 --> 03:01.566 align:middle
Last month we quickly lowered
our policy interest rate

03:01.566 --> 03:02.366 align:middle
to near zero.

03:03.156 --> 03:05.276 align:middle
We stated then and again
today that we expect

03:05.276 --> 03:08.866 align:middle
to maintain interest rates at
this level until we're confident

03:08.866 --> 03:12.096 align:middle
that the economy has weathered
recent events and is on track

03:12.096 --> 03:13.886 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

03:13.886 --> 03:15.346 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

03:16.696 --> 03:19.476 align:middle
Of course, lowering interest
rates cannot stop the sharp drop

03:19.476 --> 03:21.606 align:middle
in economic activity
caused by closures

03:21.956 --> 03:23.506 align:middle
and other forms of
social distancing.

03:24.176 --> 03:26.636 align:middle
And low rates will not
effectively spur the economy

03:26.636 --> 03:27.996 align:middle
if those rates do not feed

03:27.996 --> 03:30.816 align:middle
through to broader financial
conditions or if households

03:30.816 --> 03:32.596 align:middle
and businesses are
unable to get credit.

03:34.356 --> 03:35.806 align:middle
The economic disruptions caused

03:35.806 --> 03:38.096 align:middle
by the virus created
tremendous strains

03:38.096 --> 03:39.746 align:middle
in some essential
financial markets

03:39.786 --> 03:42.046 align:middle
and have impaired the flow
of credit in the economy.

03:43.026 --> 03:45.496 align:middle
Without access to credit,
families can be forced

03:45.596 --> 03:48.346 align:middle
to cut back on necessities
and could lose their homes.

03:49.166 --> 03:51.636 align:middle
Businesses can be forced
to downsize or close,

03:51.836 --> 03:54.276 align:middle
resulting in further
losses of jobs and incomes

03:54.276 --> 03:55.466 align:middle
and worsening the downturn.

03:56.686 --> 03:59.106 align:middle
Preserving the flow of
credit is thus essential

03:59.106 --> 04:01.006 align:middle
for mitigating the
damage to the economy

04:01.326 --> 04:02.976 align:middle
and setting the stage
for the recovery.

04:04.606 --> 04:08.316 align:middle
We have been taking broad and
forceful actions to these ends.

04:09.016 --> 04:11.306 align:middle
To support the flow of credit
to households and businesses,

04:11.456 --> 04:13.096 align:middle
foster smooth market
functioning,

04:13.416 --> 04:16.276 align:middle
and promote effective
transmission of monetary policy

04:16.276 --> 04:17.596 align:middle
to broader financial conditions,

04:18.236 --> 04:20.476 align:middle
we have been purchasing
large amounts of Treasury

04:20.476 --> 04:22.516 align:middle
and agency mortgage-backed
securities.

04:23.486 --> 04:26.016 align:middle
The markets for these
securities play a critical role

04:26.016 --> 04:29.416 align:middle
in the economy, and they came
under great stress last month

04:29.876 --> 04:32.366 align:middle
as the scale of economic
disruption became clearer

04:32.816 --> 04:34.906 align:middle
and as investors
clamored for liquidity.

04:35.946 --> 04:39.576 align:middle
Our purchases have helped market
conditions improve substantially

04:39.576 --> 04:40.406 align:middle
in recent weeks.

04:41.196 --> 04:42.296 align:middle
In light of this improvement,

04:42.296 --> 04:44.526 align:middle
we have slowed our
pace of purchases.

04:44.846 --> 04:47.366 align:middle
We will continue our
purchases of Treasury

04:47.366 --> 04:49.866 align:middle
and agency mortgage-backed
securities as needed.

04:51.326 --> 04:54.116 align:middle
While the primary purpose of
these securities purchases is

04:54.116 --> 04:55.876 align:middle
to preserve smooth
market functioning

04:55.876 --> 04:57.466 align:middle
and effective policy
transmission,

04:58.296 --> 05:01.806 align:middle
the purchases will offer-will
also foster more accommodative

05:01.806 --> 05:02.846 align:middle
financial conditions.

05:03.376 --> 05:07.116 align:middle
We are also undertaking
programs to provide stability

05:07.116 --> 05:09.916 align:middle
to the financial system and to
more directly support the flow

05:09.916 --> 05:13.296 align:middle
of credit in the economy-for
households, for businesses

05:13.296 --> 05:16.016 align:middle
of all sizes, and for state
and local governments.

05:16.966 --> 05:20.566 align:middle
These programs benefit the
economy by providing financing

05:20.566 --> 05:22.706 align:middle
where it is not otherwise
available.

05:23.086 --> 05:26.926 align:middle
In addition, by serving as a
backstop to key credit markets,

05:27.066 --> 05:29.166 align:middle
the programs can improve
market functioning

05:29.516 --> 05:30.816 align:middle
by increasing the willingness

05:30.816 --> 05:32.616 align:middle
of private lenders
to extend credit.

05:33.836 --> 05:36.766 align:middle
Many of these programs rely
on emergency lending powers

05:36.766 --> 05:39.906 align:middle
that are available only in
very unusual circumstances,

05:40.206 --> 05:42.256 align:middle
such as those we find
ourselves in today.

05:43.376 --> 05:45.076 align:middle
We are deploying
these lending powers

05:45.076 --> 05:48.416 align:middle
to an unprecedented extent,
enabled in large part

05:48.416 --> 05:50.016 align:middle
by the financial
backing and support

05:50.016 --> 05:51.506 align:middle
from Congress and the Treasury.

05:52.606 --> 05:55.876 align:middle
We will continue to use these
powers forcefully, proactively,

05:55.876 --> 05:59.166 align:middle
and aggressively until we're
confident that we are solidly

05:59.166 --> 06:00.316 align:middle
on the road to recovery.

06:00.896 --> 06:04.286 align:middle
I would stress that
these are lending powers

06:04.286 --> 06:05.686 align:middle
and not spending powers.

06:06.026 --> 06:08.836 align:middle
The Fed cannot grant money
to particular beneficiaries.

06:09.216 --> 06:11.436 align:middle
We can only make loans
to solvent entities

06:11.516 --> 06:14.616 align:middle
that the-with the expectation
that the loans will be repaid.

06:15.776 --> 06:18.626 align:middle
Many borrowers will
benefit from our programs,

06:18.696 --> 06:20.046 align:middle
as will the overall economy.

06:20.806 --> 06:23.516 align:middle
But for many others, getting
a loan that may be difficult

06:23.516 --> 06:25.456 align:middle
to repay may not be the answer.

06:26.416 --> 06:29.706 align:middle
In these cases, direct
fiscal support may be needed.

06:30.756 --> 06:33.266 align:middle
Elected officials have
the power to tax and spend

06:33.696 --> 06:36.156 align:middle
and to make decisions about
where we, as a society,

06:36.586 --> 06:38.576 align:middle
should direct our
collective resources.

06:39.636 --> 06:42.726 align:middle
The Cares Act and other
legislation provide direct help

06:42.726 --> 06:44.536 align:middle
to people, businesses,
and communities.

06:45.446 --> 06:48.896 align:middle
This direct support can make
a critical difference not just

06:48.896 --> 06:51.126 align:middle
in helping families and
businesses in a time of need,

06:51.896 --> 06:54.336 align:middle
but also in limiting
long-lasting damage

06:54.336 --> 06:55.056 align:middle
to our economy.

06:55.616 --> 06:59.986 align:middle
At the Fed, we're doing all we
can to help American families

07:00.076 --> 07:02.246 align:middle
and businesses weather
this difficult period.

07:02.796 --> 07:05.616 align:middle
When the spread of the
virus is under control,

07:06.016 --> 07:08.596 align:middle
businesses will reopen, and
people will come back to work.

07:09.576 --> 07:11.496 align:middle
We will continue to
use our tools to assure

07:11.496 --> 07:13.066 align:middle
that the recovery,
when it comes,

07:13.406 --> 07:15.236 align:middle
will be as robust as possible.

07:16.326 --> 07:18.236 align:middle
Thank you, and I'll be glad
to take your questions.

07:18.896 --> 07:19.906 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:19.906 --> 07:20.686 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek.

07:22.116 --> 07:22.896 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

07:22.896 --> 07:23.816 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

07:25.136 --> 07:28.326 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

07:28.326 --> 07:31.466 align:middle
You guys have cut rates to zero,
you're buying up huge quantities

07:31.466 --> 07:33.516 align:middle
of government debt-I
guess the question is,

07:33.516 --> 07:36.296 align:middle
what more can you realistically
do to help the economy,

07:36.296 --> 07:40.956 align:middle
and where do you see a need
for Congress to step in?

07:41.316 --> 07:41.796 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:42.186 --> 07:43.836 align:middle
So let me say that
we're-we're committed

07:43.836 --> 07:46.826 align:middle
to using our full range of
tools to support the economy

07:46.826 --> 07:47.776 align:middle
in this challenging time.

07:48.266 --> 07:50.326 align:middle
We're going to use them,
as I mentioned, forcefully,

07:50.326 --> 07:52.496 align:middle
proactively, and aggressively
until we're confident

07:52.496 --> 07:58.076 align:middle
that we're solidly on the road
to recovery and also to assure

07:58.076 --> 07:59.436 align:middle
that that recovery,
when it comes,

07:59.436 --> 08:01.436 align:middle
will be as robust as possible.

08:01.986 --> 08:03.686 align:middle
As long as needed,
we'll use them.

08:03.756 --> 08:07.586 align:middle
And I would just say, we
have a number of dimensions

08:07.676 --> 08:09.866 align:middle
on which we can still provide
support to the economy.

08:09.866 --> 08:11.826 align:middle
As you know, our credit
policies are not subject

08:11.826 --> 08:12.926 align:middle
to a specific dollar limit.

08:13.096 --> 08:15.236 align:middle
They can be expanded
as appropriate,

08:15.236 --> 08:17.236 align:middle
and we can do new ones.

08:17.626 --> 08:20.246 align:middle
So we can continue to
be part of the answer.

08:21.016 --> 08:22.656 align:middle
Will there be a need
to do more, though?

08:22.656 --> 08:25.286 align:middle
I think the answer to
that-that will be "yes."

08:25.336 --> 08:29.746 align:middle
I would say, Congress has also
reacted quite aggressively

08:29.966 --> 08:31.496 align:middle
and strongly with the Cares Act

08:32.236 --> 08:35.076 align:middle
and other laws-several
other laws.

08:35.536 --> 08:36.376 align:middle
And that's appropriate.

08:36.646 --> 08:38.846 align:middle
With enhanced unemployment
insurance

08:38.846 --> 08:41.336 align:middle
and the Paycheck
Protection Program,

08:41.336 --> 08:44.366 align:middle
we've seen an extraordinary,
historically large reaction.

08:45.186 --> 08:48.356 align:middle
But I would say that
it may well be the case

08:48.446 --> 08:51.986 align:middle
that the economy will need
more support from all of us

08:52.346 --> 08:54.566 align:middle
if the-if the recovery
is to be a robust one.

08:55.046 --> 08:59.466 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

08:59.466 --> 09:00.256 align:middle
Nick Timiraos.

09:01.186 --> 09:03.366 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

09:03.366 --> 09:04.666 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

09:04.666 --> 09:06.466 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

09:06.466 --> 09:09.926 align:middle
I want to follow up on
Jeanna's question and ask,

09:09.926 --> 09:14.346 align:middle
what specifically do you
think elected officials should

09:14.346 --> 09:17.606 align:middle
consider in order to help return

09:18.016 --> 09:21.016 align:middle
or hasten a stronger
return to full employment?

09:21.596 --> 09:25.736 align:middle
And what policy tools did you
discuss at your meeting today

09:26.196 --> 09:30.006 align:middle
that might be used to stop
a deflationary spiral?

09:31.316 --> 09:32.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:34.466 --> 09:37.056 align:middle
So let me-let me
start-I'll take the questions

09:37.056 --> 09:38.356 align:middle
in the order you-you gave them.

09:39.156 --> 09:44.956 align:middle
So in terms of, if I can
say, legislative priorities,

09:45.076 --> 09:47.306 align:middle
we don't-we're not
responsible for fiscal policy.

09:47.306 --> 09:48.986 align:middle
Those will be decisions
for Congress to make.

09:49.596 --> 09:53.096 align:middle
But I would say that
policies that protect workers,

09:53.096 --> 09:56.586 align:middle
businesses, and households
from avoidable insolvency,

09:56.586 --> 09:59.236 align:middle
that keep businesses
going so that they're able

09:59.236 --> 10:02.436 align:middle
to produce goods
and-and to either hold

10:02.436 --> 10:04.286 align:middle
on to their employees
or quickly rehire them,

10:04.286 --> 10:06.246 align:middle
those are going to
be key policies.

10:06.636 --> 10:10.126 align:middle
They'll come with a hefty
price tag, but we would come

10:10.126 --> 10:13.586 align:middle
out of this event eventually
with-with a stronger economy

10:13.586 --> 10:15.896 align:middle
and with less long-run
damage to the economy.

10:15.896 --> 10:20.226 align:middle
So that's-that's a key
thing that-that, really,

10:21.356 --> 10:23.026 align:middle
Congress could do over time.

10:23.566 --> 10:25.906 align:middle
In terms of what we
can do, I mentioned,

10:26.406 --> 10:29.136 align:middle
we have our-our credit
facilities are-are wide open.

10:29.136 --> 10:30.216 align:middle
We can do more on that front.

10:30.576 --> 10:31.496 align:middle
In addition, you know,

10:31.496 --> 10:34.306 align:middle
we've had-we've had
extensive discussions,

10:34.306 --> 10:36.226 align:middle
as I've mentioned-done
a lot of thinking

10:36.226 --> 10:39.716 align:middle
about what monetary policy might
look like over-in coming months

10:39.716 --> 10:44.486 align:middle
over a range of potential
scenarios for the economy.

10:45.046 --> 10:48.716 align:middle
We do think that our-our
policy stance today is right

10:48.716 --> 10:50.336 align:middle
where it should be for now.

10:50.996 --> 10:53.536 align:middle
As you know, we cut rates to
the effective lower bound,

10:53.886 --> 10:56.786 align:middle
we-and we've said that
we'll keep them there

10:57.336 --> 11:01.186 align:middle
until we're confident that
the economy has weathered the

11:01.186 --> 11:04.156 align:middle
effects of the outbreak and is
on track to achieve our goals.

11:04.856 --> 11:07.006 align:middle
With asset purchases,
we're continuing

11:07.006 --> 11:10.176 align:middle
to purchase Treasury
securities and agency MBS

11:10.326 --> 11:14.376 align:middle
in the amounts needed to support
smooth market functioning.

11:15.026 --> 11:18.996 align:middle
So we're doing those things, and
I would say that we'll continue

11:18.996 --> 11:21.116 align:middle
to use our tools as needs be.

11:21.116 --> 11:21.826 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:25.226 --> 11:27.056 align:middle
Steve Liesman.

11:27.246 --> 11:28.776 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

11:28.776 --> 11:32.676 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, question-Steve
Liesman with CNBC.

11:32.676 --> 11:33.796 align:middle
I've got two questions here.

11:33.796 --> 11:36.076 align:middle
First of all, why
aren't you announcing

11:36.256 --> 11:39.876 align:middle
or conducting a specific
quantitative easing program?

11:40.546 --> 11:43.706 align:middle
And-and maybe you could
explain what the context is now

11:43.706 --> 11:45.546 align:middle
of the purchases
that will be made

11:45.546 --> 11:46.686 align:middle
and the amounts that
are to come.

11:46.786 --> 11:48.666 align:middle
The second question I have is,

11:48.666 --> 11:51.566 align:middle
are-is the Federal Reserve
really taking enough risk

11:51.566 --> 11:54.116 align:middle
to help households
and businesses here,

11:54.116 --> 11:57.656 align:middle
in that you just said
that the programs that are

11:57.656 --> 12:01.016 align:middle
out there may be best for those
who are able to repay loans.

12:01.016 --> 12:02.716 align:middle
But-so don't you have a series

12:02.716 --> 12:06.576 align:middle
of programs essentially lending
only to those able to repay,

12:06.936 --> 12:09.276 align:middle
which is essentially helping
those who may need it the least?

12:09.276 --> 12:09.706 align:middle
Thank you.

12:11.246 --> 12:12.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:12.246 --> 12:17.586 align:middle
So, let me say again, in terms
of-of asset purchases and-and

12:17.586 --> 12:20.776 align:middle
other measures, we-we do
think our-our policy stance is

12:20.776 --> 12:21.676 align:middle
appropriate now.

12:22.446 --> 12:25.236 align:middle
We've had discussions, as
I've mentioned, over time

12:25.236 --> 12:26.366 align:middle
and done quite a bit of thinking

12:26.366 --> 12:27.746 align:middle
about what we might
do in the future.

12:27.746 --> 12:30.026 align:middle
We think, for now, our
policy stance is appropriate.

12:30.026 --> 12:31.746 align:middle
We're not going to
change it now.

12:32.276 --> 12:34.806 align:middle
And we're really waiting to
see more from the economy.

12:34.806 --> 12:38.646 align:middle
There are a range of potential
paths the economy could be on,

12:38.646 --> 12:43.996 align:middle
and I think as-as the time
approaches for-then we'll-you

12:43.996 --> 12:45.796 align:middle
know, we'll address
your first question,

12:46.456 --> 12:48.076 align:middle
which is about asset purchases.

12:48.076 --> 12:49.926 align:middle
So-but that's not
something we're doing today.

12:50.056 --> 12:55.526 align:middle
It's-it's something that we have
talked about, and, you know,

12:55.526 --> 12:57.876 align:middle
for now, we like our
current policy stance.

12:58.386 --> 13:01.256 align:middle
In terms of risk, so, you know,

13:01.256 --> 13:07.316 align:middle
we operate under the laws
that Congress passes.

13:07.596 --> 13:10.436 align:middle
And there are a number of
aspects under section 13(3),

13:10.436 --> 13:12.506 align:middle
and you can see that they've
permitted us to, I think,

13:12.576 --> 13:15.576 align:middle
move very quickly
and move into areas

13:15.576 --> 13:19.056 align:middle
where we've never been before
and do so quite aggressively.

13:19.206 --> 13:24.786 align:middle
So I-I think we're going places
and providing help in places

13:24.786 --> 13:27.566 align:middle
where we never have, and
I'm glad that we are.

13:27.566 --> 13:28.946 align:middle
I think it's appropriate
that we are.

13:29.536 --> 13:32.266 align:middle
Nonetheless, these
are lending powers.

13:32.386 --> 13:35.886 align:middle
And if you read section
13(3) of the Dodd-Frank-or,

13:35.886 --> 13:38.336 align:middle
the Federal Reserve Act,
and I know you have,

13:38.836 --> 13:43.556 align:middle
it does require that-that we
be secured to our satisfaction,

13:44.196 --> 13:48.246 align:middle
and-and we can't lend
to insolvent companies.

13:48.616 --> 13:50.546 align:middle
You know, it's clear
these are lending powers,

13:50.546 --> 13:54.676 align:middle
and we are-we are ultimately
bound to-to implement the laws

13:54.676 --> 13:55.916 align:middle
that Congress gives us.

13:56.476 --> 13:57.636 align:middle
We-we do not make grants.

13:57.636 --> 13:58.516 align:middle
We can't make grants.

13:58.516 --> 14:01.946 align:middle
And I-the reason I raised
that in my remarks is

14:01.946 --> 14:03.316 align:middle
that I just want to
be clear on that.

14:03.316 --> 14:05.986 align:middle
We-you know, we can do what
we can do, and we will do it

14:06.056 --> 14:07.766 align:middle
to the absolute limit
of those powers.

14:07.766 --> 14:10.116 align:middle
We will-we'll keep at it.

14:10.116 --> 14:13.286 align:middle
And you-I just want people
to know that we'll-we will be

14:13.286 --> 14:15.546 align:middle
at it with-with the legal
authorities that we have

14:15.546 --> 14:16.996 align:middle
until we get through this thing.

14:16.996 --> 14:18.406 align:middle
We will keep using
our authorities.

14:19.076 --> 14:21.286 align:middle
But there are authorities
that we don't have,

14:21.536 --> 14:23.746 align:middle
and there may be a need
for those authorities

14:23.746 --> 14:25.266 align:middle
to be used as well as ours.

14:25.346 --> 14:25.946 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:29.126 --> 14:29.896 align:middle
Heather Long.

14:31.866 --> 14:35.946 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

14:36.436 --> 14:38.766 align:middle
Hi. Hi, Chair Powell,
it's Heather Long

14:38.766 --> 14:39.956 align:middle
from the Washington Post.

14:40.026 --> 14:44.296 align:middle
I have two questions
for you, both on timing.

14:44.636 --> 14:48.236 align:middle
The first, do you plan to launch
the Corporate Credit Facility

14:48.236 --> 14:51.776 align:middle
and the Main Street lending
facilities in May or early May?

14:51.776 --> 14:54.366 align:middle
Anything you can give
us-a sense on timing?

14:54.366 --> 14:56.476 align:middle
And the second, in
the statement,

14:56.476 --> 14:59.646 align:middle
you referred today-the
FOMC refers today to a lot

14:59.646 --> 15:02.786 align:middle
of medium-term risks
from the pandemic.

15:03.326 --> 15:05.316 align:middle
That sounds like you
all think this is going

15:05.316 --> 15:08.516 align:middle
to be a long recovery-a
long road to recovery.

15:08.516 --> 15:10.396 align:middle
Can you give any more guidance

15:10.396 --> 15:13.136 align:middle
on how you all see this
recovery taking shape?

15:13.136 --> 15:14.356 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

15:14.386 --> 15:16.956 align:middle
Sure. So, in terms
of those facilities,

15:16.956 --> 15:23.486 align:middle
the corporate credit facilities
are near being finalized

15:23.486 --> 15:27.126 align:middle
and will be operating, I
would say, soon-fairly soon.

15:27.576 --> 15:31.946 align:middle
Main Street facility
is-we're close

15:31.946 --> 15:35.436 align:middle
to announcing-to
issuing a new term sheet.

15:35.436 --> 15:38.946 align:middle
As you know, we put out a term
sheet for comment a while back,

15:38.946 --> 15:40.716 align:middle
and we got a couple
thousand comments.

15:40.716 --> 15:42.306 align:middle
We've carefully studied them.

15:42.916 --> 15:45.416 align:middle
We've tried to reflect those
in what we're doing now.

15:45.776 --> 15:49.256 align:middle
I think, with Main Street,
there'll be at least a couple

15:49.326 --> 15:52.976 align:middle
of different kinds of
lending going on there.

15:53.016 --> 15:55.936 align:middle
It-this is a broad
area of the economy

15:55.936 --> 15:58.376 align:middle
with many different kinds of
credit needs, so we're going

15:58.376 --> 16:00.946 align:middle
to keep at that for
some time, I think,

16:01.066 --> 16:03.646 align:middle
adding in sectors
and lending products.

16:03.646 --> 16:06.676 align:middle
So I think we'll be at-we'll
probably be continuing to work

16:06.676 --> 16:08.526 align:middle
and expand Main Street
for some time,

16:08.526 --> 16:10.376 align:middle
but it won't be done
quite as quickly.

16:10.836 --> 16:11.966 align:middle
But the first part
of it, I think,

16:11.966 --> 16:13.136 align:middle
will be done fairly quickly.

16:13.866 --> 16:17.036 align:middle
In terms of our statement,
so what we said was

16:17.036 --> 16:19.396 align:middle
that we-I'll just
read the sentence.

16:19.396 --> 16:21.566 align:middle
It says: "[The] ongoing public
health crisis will weigh heavily

16:21.606 --> 16:23.576 align:middle
on economic activity,
employment, and inflation

16:23.576 --> 16:26.456 align:middle
in the near term and
poses considerable risks

16:26.456 --> 16:28.546 align:middle
to the economic outlook
over the medium term."

16:29.276 --> 16:31.436 align:middle
So the "medium"-but
what we meant by that,

16:31.436 --> 16:34.246 align:middle
"over the medium term"-so,
obviously, what we're talking

16:34.246 --> 16:37.506 align:middle
about is, you know, not right
now, in the very near term.

16:37.506 --> 16:40.116 align:middle
It's between now and the long
term, so the next year or so.

16:40.836 --> 16:44.296 align:middle
And I would point to
a couple of-of risks

16:44.296 --> 16:47.376 align:middle
to the outlook that-what
we were thinking of.

16:47.726 --> 16:48.936 align:middle
First is just the virus.

16:49.266 --> 16:51.596 align:middle
How long will it take
to get it under control?

16:51.596 --> 16:53.276 align:middle
Will there be additional
outbreaks?

16:53.546 --> 16:56.616 align:middle
Will there be drugs that can
either treat it or-or a vaccine

16:56.616 --> 16:57.136 align:middle
of some kind?

16:57.136 --> 17:00.986 align:middle
So there's-all of that is very
much shrouded in uncertainty.

17:01.676 --> 17:07.776 align:middle
The second issue-and this is a
very substantial one-is just the

17:07.856 --> 17:12.456 align:middle
possibility of-of damage
to the productive capacity

17:12.456 --> 17:14.676 align:middle
of the economy through
a couple of channels.

17:15.186 --> 17:19.926 align:middle
The first is just workers
who-if one is unemployed

17:20.326 --> 17:25.546 align:middle
for an extended period, that
person can-can lose the skills

17:25.546 --> 17:27.776 align:middle
that are needed, can lose
touch with the labor force,

17:28.286 --> 17:31.096 align:middle
and have difficulty
restarting his or her career.

17:31.516 --> 17:34.786 align:middle
So that's a-that's a feature
of deep and long recessions,

17:34.786 --> 17:36.266 align:middle
and that's something we've
got to watch out for.

17:36.776 --> 17:39.776 align:middle
Another is just businesses-you
know, these thousands

17:39.776 --> 17:42.066 align:middle
of great medium- and
small-sized businesses

17:42.066 --> 17:43.336 align:middle
that we have all
over the country.

17:43.826 --> 17:45.986 align:middle
They're worth so much
more to the economy

17:45.986 --> 17:47.786 align:middle
than the sum of their
net assets.

17:48.196 --> 17:50.456 align:middle
They contain-you know,
they're job creators,

17:50.456 --> 17:51.426 align:middle
they're really important.

17:51.426 --> 17:54.746 align:middle
And if we see an
unnecessary-unnecessary

17:54.746 --> 17:57.706 align:middle
insolvencies, a wave of
those, that could be damaging

17:57.706 --> 17:59.806 align:middle
to the performance of
the economy over time.

17:59.806 --> 18:02.546 align:middle
So the good news is that we
have policies, as I mentioned

18:02.546 --> 18:04.206 align:middle
in my remarks-we have policies

18:04.206 --> 18:07.336 align:middle
that can address those
things, but not perfectly.

18:07.396 --> 18:09.136 align:middle
So that's-that's another risk.

18:09.536 --> 18:12.076 align:middle
The third thing I would point
to is just the global dimension.

18:12.626 --> 18:14.826 align:middle
This is a very global
phenomenon,

18:14.826 --> 18:19.446 align:middle
and you are-we're
seeing economic data

18:19.446 --> 18:23.536 align:middle
from around the globe which is
very, very negative, and that,

18:23.536 --> 18:26.836 align:middle
too, can weigh on U.S.
economic performance over time.

18:27.586 --> 18:31.226 align:middle
I-you know, I would say, just
on the U.S.-on the U.S. economy,

18:32.226 --> 18:34.416 align:middle
there are things you
can say, one can say.

18:34.416 --> 18:37.566 align:middle
The first is that, in the
near term, we're going to see,

18:37.956 --> 18:40.956 align:middle
you know, significant
declines in economic activity,

18:40.956 --> 18:43.216 align:middle
significant declines
in employment,

18:43.216 --> 18:44.586 align:middle
and increases in unemployment.

18:44.586 --> 18:47.296 align:middle
We're going to see that as
a consequence of the virus

18:47.296 --> 18:49.276 align:middle
and the measures we're taking
to protect ourselves from it.

18:50.666 --> 18:54.906 align:middle
The next phases are more
uncertain, highly uncertain,

18:54.906 --> 18:58.026 align:middle
but we will go through a
phase starting fairly soon

18:58.026 --> 18:59.726 align:middle
where we begin to
reopen the economy.

18:59.796 --> 19:03.356 align:middle
And probably the economic
activity will pick

19:03.356 --> 19:05.186 align:middle
up as consumer spending
picks up.

19:05.216 --> 19:06.436 align:middle
Consumer spending,
as I mentioned,

19:06.436 --> 19:07.606 align:middle
has gone down quite a lot.

19:08.216 --> 19:10.526 align:middle
It'll begin to pick up
as people start to return

19:10.526 --> 19:13.286 align:middle
to their normal patterns
of spending.

19:13.436 --> 19:15.946 align:middle
But the chances are that
it won't go right back

19:15.946 --> 19:18.896 align:middle
to where we were, because people
will-until they're confident

19:19.356 --> 19:21.866 align:middle
of-that the virus is well
and truly under control,

19:22.436 --> 19:26.716 align:middle
then they will be somewhat
probably reluctant to-to

19:26.716 --> 19:28.346 align:middle
undertake certain
kinds of activity.

19:28.346 --> 19:31.056 align:middle
So it may take some time for us

19:31.056 --> 19:33.046 align:middle
to get back-it probably
will take some time for us

19:33.046 --> 19:35.996 align:middle
to get back to-you know,
to a more normal level

19:35.996 --> 19:38.136 align:middle
of unemployment and-and,
ultimately,

19:38.136 --> 19:39.016 align:middle
to maximum employment.

19:39.076 --> 19:39.626 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:43.056 --> 19:44.166 align:middle
Okay, James Politi.

19:44.166 --> 19:45.396 align:middle
JAMES POLITI.

19:45.396 --> 19:47.866 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

19:47.866 --> 19:51.946 align:middle
You didn't firm up your
guidance on-this is James Politi

19:51.946 --> 19:54.526 align:middle
from the Financial Times-you
didn't firm up your guidance

19:54.526 --> 19:56.666 align:middle
on interest rates
at this meeting.

19:56.666 --> 19:58.786 align:middle
And given the risks
that you just outlined,

19:59.096 --> 20:02.146 align:middle
under what circumstances
would you strengthen the Fed's

20:02.146 --> 20:04.976 align:middle
commitment to keep rates
at the lower bound?

20:04.976 --> 20:08.776 align:middle
And is there any danger to
delaying-delaying that plan?

20:08.776 --> 20:10.436 align:middle
Then, on the credit facilities,

20:10.716 --> 20:13.726 align:middle
what kind of demand are you
expecting for the programs

20:13.896 --> 20:18.006 align:middle
that were set up under the Cares
Act, and are you expecting them

20:18.006 --> 20:20.546 align:middle
to rapidly reach capacity
like the PPP plan did?

20:20.766 --> 20:22.786 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:22.786 --> 20:26.316 align:middle
Okay. So on the first,
as I mentioned, you know,

20:26.316 --> 20:28.816 align:middle
we moved very quickly,
very aggressively.

20:28.816 --> 20:30.136 align:middle
We were the first to return

20:30.136 --> 20:34.146 align:middle
to the effective lower
bound, where we are now.

20:34.146 --> 20:36.346 align:middle
We got there essentially
right away,

20:36.906 --> 20:39.096 align:middle
and we think that's
the right place to be.

20:39.096 --> 20:40.586 align:middle
We think, if you
look at surveys,

20:40.586 --> 20:45.316 align:middle
you look at market pricing, the
market expects us to-to be there

20:45.316 --> 20:47.726 align:middle
for a good while, and
that's appropriate.

20:48.256 --> 20:52.116 align:middle
So, you know, it's not as
though the market is pricing

20:52.116 --> 20:54.656 align:middle
in a near-term liftoff
or anything like that.

20:54.656 --> 20:58.706 align:middle
And let me just say, we're
going to not be in any hurry

20:58.786 --> 21:00.786 align:middle
to withdraw these
measures or to lift off.

21:00.786 --> 21:04.026 align:middle
We're going to wait until
we're-we're quite confident

21:04.496 --> 21:08.006 align:middle
that the economy is-is well
on the road to recovery.

21:08.006 --> 21:12.186 align:middle
So we don't see-we
see our current stance

21:12.186 --> 21:14.216 align:middle
and our current guidance-and
I would say the same thing

21:14.216 --> 21:16.826 align:middle
about asset purchases-we
see them as appropriate.

21:17.516 --> 21:19.836 align:middle
I've-I've also mentioned
now a couple times

21:19.836 --> 21:21.376 align:middle
that we have done
a lot of thinking

21:21.376 --> 21:25.336 align:middle
about what monetary policy might
look like over coming months,

21:25.796 --> 21:30.266 align:middle
and that would, you know, depend
on-on where we are in-in a range

21:30.266 --> 21:33.316 align:middle
of potential economic scenarios.

21:33.636 --> 21:36.106 align:middle
So we-we were thinking
about that all the time,

21:36.106 --> 21:39.576 align:middle
but right now, for now, we
think our current stance

21:39.576 --> 21:40.166 align:middle
is appropriate.

21:40.166 --> 21:43.806 align:middle
So we made no change
in it today.

21:43.806 --> 21:52.696 align:middle
Second question was, how-so the
thing about our facilities is,

21:54.226 --> 21:56.306 align:middle
Treasury still has
plenty of equity.

21:56.886 --> 22:03.446 align:middle
So we've said that if-if demand
for our facilities is greater

22:03.446 --> 22:05.956 align:middle
than we've estimated,
then we'll expand them.

22:06.216 --> 22:07.416 align:middle
And we have the ability
to do that.

22:07.476 --> 22:13.516 align:middle
So it won't be the way the-the
Paycheck Protection Program is,

22:13.516 --> 22:17.086 align:middle
where there's a specific amount
allocated-appropriated for it,

22:17.186 --> 22:19.136 align:middle
and-and then there's
no more money.

22:19.136 --> 22:21.406 align:middle
That's-that will be unlikely

22:21.406 --> 22:24.006 align:middle
to happen here unless we
exhaust Treasury's equity,

22:24.006 --> 22:25.406 align:middle
and we're a long
way from doing that.

22:26.076 --> 22:29.296 align:middle
The second thing I would say
is-and you've all seen this-is

22:29.366 --> 22:32.636 align:middle
when we announce these
facilities-and I mentioned this

22:32.636 --> 22:35.216 align:middle
in my remarks-it's not just
the actual lending we do,

22:35.216 --> 22:37.496 align:middle
it's the-we build
confidence in the market.

22:38.556 --> 22:40.956 align:middle
And private market
participants come in.

22:40.956 --> 22:43.616 align:middle
And many companies that
would've had to come

22:43.616 --> 22:46.916 align:middle
to the Fed have now been able
to finance themselves privately

22:47.176 --> 22:51.136 align:middle
since we announced the-the
initial term sheet on-on

22:51.186 --> 22:52.206 align:middle
these facilities.

22:52.206 --> 22:53.686 align:middle
So that's a good thing.

22:53.716 --> 22:55.116 align:middle
Companies are out
there financing,

22:55.116 --> 22:56.526 align:middle
they're out there
raising liquidity.

22:56.526 --> 22:59.526 align:middle
We haven't made any corporate
loans to-in those facilities.

22:59.526 --> 23:01.136 align:middle
We've made the short-term
money market loans,

23:01.136 --> 23:03.076 align:middle
but-we haven't made any of them.

23:03.606 --> 23:06.296 align:middle
And yet there's a tremendous
amount of financing going on,

23:06.296 --> 23:07.556 align:middle
and that's a good thing.

23:07.556 --> 23:11.256 align:middle
So we-for that reason,
the ultimate demand

23:11.256 --> 23:14.016 align:middle
for the facilities is
quite difficult to predict,

23:14.016 --> 23:15.806 align:middle
because there is this
announcement effect

23:16.236 --> 23:19.216 align:middle
that really gets the
market functioning again.

23:19.246 --> 23:21.446 align:middle
Of course, we have to
follow through, though.

23:21.506 --> 23:25.076 align:middle
And we will follow through
to validate that-that

23:25.076 --> 23:25.776 align:middle
announcement effect.

23:25.886 --> 23:26.346 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:27.796 --> 23:28.966 align:middle
Okay, Howard Schneider.

23:29.806 --> 23:30.236 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

23:30.896 --> 23:32.976 align:middle
Oh, hi, Chair Powell, and
thanks for doing this.

23:32.976 --> 23:36.196 align:middle
I wanted to get your-a little
more expansion on the timing

23:36.196 --> 23:38.366 align:middle
of recovery and the-the
connection

23:38.366 --> 23:40.806 align:middle
between the health response.

23:40.806 --> 23:43.246 align:middle
You said earlier, I think, that
you thought the second half

23:43.246 --> 23:45.346 align:middle
of the year could see a
pretty robust rebound.

23:45.346 --> 23:47.556 align:middle
Is that now out of the question?

23:47.556 --> 23:50.936 align:middle
And do you think a steady
recovery is really possible

23:51.446 --> 23:54.316 align:middle
until a vaccine's
developed, given the patchwork

23:54.316 --> 23:57.016 align:middle
of measures we've seen
taken around the states?

23:57.016 --> 23:58.266 align:middle
Is that going to really lead us

23:58.266 --> 24:01.716 align:middle
to a steady path out,
absent a vaccine?

24:02.386 --> 24:02.916 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:04.016 --> 24:07.626 align:middle
You know, as you know, economic
forecasts are always uncertain.

24:08.376 --> 24:10.006 align:middle
Today they're unusually
uncertain,

24:10.006 --> 24:13.826 align:middle
and that's-that's really because
so much of the performance

24:13.826 --> 24:15.746 align:middle
of the economy depends
on the path of the virus

24:15.746 --> 24:17.896 align:middle
and the success of the
measures we take to control it,

24:18.336 --> 24:21.066 align:middle
our success in reopening
the economy,

24:21.066 --> 24:23.116 align:middle
and also the time it takes
to develop new drugs.

24:23.956 --> 24:25.696 align:middle
And our tools, the
things that we do,

24:25.696 --> 24:27.236 align:middle
don't affect any
of those things.

24:27.236 --> 24:29.256 align:middle
We're not experts on
those things either,

24:29.256 --> 24:31.306 align:middle
but what the experts tell us is

24:31.306 --> 24:34.096 align:middle
that the outcomes
are highly uncertain.

24:34.576 --> 24:37.796 align:middle
So this is an unusual, new kind
of uncertainty added on top

24:37.796 --> 24:40.256 align:middle
of our-our regular uncertainty.

24:40.306 --> 24:42.556 align:middle
But I-I will say, there-I
think there are a few things

24:42.556 --> 24:44.986 align:middle
that you can say
about-about the path ahead.

24:45.666 --> 24:48.856 align:middle
First, this time, now,
is going to be a time

24:48.856 --> 24:53.426 align:middle
of sharp contraction in economic
activity, high unemployment.

24:53.966 --> 24:58.126 align:middle
Personal consumption
expenditures have declined

24:58.126 --> 24:59.916 align:middle
sharply, business
investment as well.

24:59.916 --> 25:00.866 align:middle
Unemployment moved up.

25:00.866 --> 25:03.546 align:middle
We're going to see economic data

25:03.546 --> 25:05.056 align:middle
for the second quarter
that's worse

25:05.056 --> 25:07.236 align:middle
than any data we've
seen for the economy.

25:07.526 --> 25:10.286 align:middle
And they're a direct consequence
of the disease and the measures

25:10.286 --> 25:11.826 align:middle
that we're taking to
protect ourselves from it.

25:12.856 --> 25:15.026 align:middle
So then we will enter
this new phase,

25:15.026 --> 25:17.616 align:middle
and we-we're just
beginning to maybe do that,

25:18.066 --> 25:20.796 align:middle
where we will-formal measures

25:20.796 --> 25:25.546 align:middle
that require social distancing
will be rolled back gradually

25:26.046 --> 25:28.596 align:middle
and at different paces in
different parts of the country.

25:29.206 --> 25:32.256 align:middle
And, in time, this-during
this period,

25:32.256 --> 25:33.686 align:middle
the economy will
begin to recover.

25:34.356 --> 25:36.596 align:middle
People will come out of their
homes, start to spend again.

25:36.596 --> 25:38.476 align:middle
We'll see unemployment go down,

25:38.646 --> 25:40.356 align:middle
we'll see economic
activity pick up.

25:41.176 --> 25:43.626 align:middle
And, you know, when
will that be?

25:43.626 --> 25:44.606 align:middle
It's very hard to say.

25:44.606 --> 25:46.336 align:middle
But let's just say,
for this purpose,

25:46.336 --> 25:48.146 align:middle
that it's in the third quarter.

25:48.616 --> 25:52.166 align:middle
So, as I mentioned earlier,
that-that could be a fairly,

25:52.166 --> 25:53.966 align:middle
you know, a large increase.

25:53.966 --> 25:56.526 align:middle
Given the-given the
size of the fall,

25:56.716 --> 25:58.896 align:middle
the increase could also
be substantially large,

25:59.456 --> 26:02.136 align:middle
although it's unlikely that
it would bring us quickly,

26:02.686 --> 26:04.746 align:middle
quickly all the way back
to pre-crisis levels.

26:05.126 --> 26:08.426 align:middle
Of course, this is the period
as well that carries the risk

26:08.426 --> 26:09.716 align:middle
of new outbreaks of the virus,

26:09.716 --> 26:11.236 align:middle
something we really
want to avoid.

26:11.846 --> 26:14.786 align:middle
I think then, after that period,

26:14.786 --> 26:16.716 align:middle
at some point you
will have-you know,

26:16.716 --> 26:19.366 align:middle
the kind of formal
social-distancing measures

26:19.366 --> 26:23.106 align:middle
will-will be gone, but you'll
still be left with, probably,

26:23.106 --> 26:25.826 align:middle
a level of caution
on the part of people

26:25.826 --> 26:30.406 align:middle
who will-who will worry
and probably keep worrying

26:30.406 --> 26:31.706 align:middle
for some-for some time.

26:31.706 --> 26:33.346 align:middle
You would think that
behavior will change

26:33.346 --> 26:34.626 align:middle
as people gain confidence.

26:34.626 --> 26:37.226 align:middle
So the-the sooner we get
the virus under control,

26:37.786 --> 26:41.806 align:middle
the sooner people can
regain that confidence

26:41.806 --> 26:44.196 align:middle
and regain their
economic activity.

26:44.256 --> 26:46.476 align:middle
I think trying to be
really precise about when

26:46.476 --> 26:48.246 align:middle
that might happen and what
the numbers might look

26:48.246 --> 26:51.446 align:middle
like is-I think it's
very tough to do that.

26:51.486 --> 26:52.086 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:53.946 --> 26:55.146 align:middle
Okay, Steve Matthews.

26:55.146 --> 26:55.816 align:middle
STEVE MATTHEWS.

26:57.116 --> 26:59.216 align:middle
Steve Matthews with Bloomberg.

26:59.216 --> 27:02.256 align:middle
Chairman Powell, you've
noted a number of times

27:02.256 --> 27:05.176 align:middle
over the last year that
there's been a broadening

27:05.176 --> 27:09.706 align:middle
out of job gains with
marginalized workers,

27:09.756 --> 27:11.756 align:middle
people who have been
left out of the recovery

27:11.756 --> 27:16.506 align:middle
over the 10-year period,
finally making job gains,

27:16.506 --> 27:20.786 align:middle
and-particularly minorities,
but also many other workers.

27:21.166 --> 27:24.886 align:middle
Do you worry that this recession
is going to fall hardest

27:24.976 --> 27:30.486 align:middle
on those workers who've
struggled and just got job gains

27:30.486 --> 27:34.036 align:middle
in the last year or two,
and that it may take years

27:34.036 --> 27:38.016 align:middle
from now before there are
opportunities for them again?

27:40.286 --> 27:40.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:40.586 --> 27:43.336 align:middle
Yes. That's exactly
what I worry about.

27:44.436 --> 27:49.936 align:middle
So unemployment has tended to
go up much faster for minorities

27:50.586 --> 27:53.936 align:middle
and for others who are-tend
to be at the low end

27:53.936 --> 27:54.906 align:middle
of the income spectrum.

27:54.906 --> 27:57.736 align:middle
And it tends to come
down-come down faster as well,

27:57.736 --> 28:01.306 align:middle
but it tends to go up faster
and be much, much higher.

28:01.986 --> 28:04.266 align:middle
And we were in a
place-only two months ago,

28:05.016 --> 28:09.056 align:middle
we were well into-beginning the
second half of the 11th year is

28:09.056 --> 28:13.006 align:middle
where we were, and every reason
to think that it was ongoing.

28:13.446 --> 28:15.646 align:middle
We were hearing from
minority-low-

28:15.646 --> 28:17.536 align:middle
and moderate-income and
minority communities

28:17.536 --> 28:19.436 align:middle
that this was the best
labor market they'd seen

28:19.436 --> 28:20.286 align:middle
in their lifetime.

28:20.706 --> 28:22.506 align:middle
All the data supported
that as well.

28:23.096 --> 28:24.526 align:middle
And it is heartbreaking,
frankly,

28:24.526 --> 28:28.056 align:middle
to see that all threatened now.

28:28.746 --> 28:33.606 align:middle
All the more need for our
urgent response-and also

28:33.606 --> 28:38.016 align:middle
that of Congress, which has
been urgent and large-and

28:38.156 --> 28:41.166 align:middle
to do what we can to
avoid longer-run damage

28:41.166 --> 28:43.396 align:middle
to the economy, which is
what I-I mentioned earlier.

28:44.956 --> 28:50.376 align:middle
This is an exogenous
event that-you know,

28:50.416 --> 28:52.206 align:middle
just, it happened to us.

28:52.206 --> 28:53.666 align:middle
It wasn't because there
was something wrong

28:53.666 --> 28:54.446 align:middle
with the economy.

28:55.246 --> 29:02.016 align:middle
And I think it is important that
we do everything we can to avoid

29:02.016 --> 29:04.596 align:middle
that longer-run damage and try
to get back to where we were,

29:04.596 --> 29:08.106 align:middle
because I do very much
have that concern.

29:08.106 --> 29:12.246 align:middle
I think everyone is suffering
here, but I think those

29:12.246 --> 29:16.636 align:middle
who are least able to-to bear it
are the ones who are, you know,

29:16.636 --> 29:20.206 align:middle
losing their jobs and
losing their incomes

29:20.206 --> 29:23.256 align:middle
and have little cushion
to-you know,

29:23.346 --> 29:25.776 align:middle
to protect them in
times like that.

29:26.326 --> 29:27.756 align:middle
So, yes, that's a
very big concern.

29:29.226 --> 29:30.286 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

29:30.286 --> 29:32.556 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

29:33.216 --> 29:35.756 align:middle
Hi, I'm Victoria
Guida with Politico.

29:35.756 --> 29:39.476 align:middle
The Paycheck Protection Program
has experienced some logistical

29:39.476 --> 29:42.246 align:middle
problems because of the speed at
which all of this is happening.

29:42.246 --> 29:46.206 align:middle
For the Main Street lending
facilities, which will also work

29:46.206 --> 29:50.226 align:middle
through banks, what lessons
are you taking away from that?

29:50.226 --> 29:52.746 align:middle
And then, more broadly,
you know,

29:52.746 --> 29:57.096 align:middle
you mentioned earlier this
year that the federal debt was

29:57.096 --> 29:58.796 align:middle
on an unsustainable path.

29:58.796 --> 30:01.556 align:middle
And I was just wondering, for
Republicans that are starting

30:01.556 --> 30:03.976 align:middle
to get worried about how much
fiscal spending they're having

30:03.976 --> 30:06.176 align:middle
to do in this crisis, you know,

30:06.176 --> 30:09.436 align:middle
whether that should
be a concern for them.

30:09.706 --> 30:10.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:10.706 --> 30:11.896 align:middle
So a couple things.

30:13.306 --> 30:15.596 align:middle
This is different from the PPP,

30:15.596 --> 30:18.736 align:middle
the Paycheck Protection
Program, in two ways.

30:18.736 --> 30:22.156 align:middle
One is, these are not
grants, these are loans.

30:22.606 --> 30:24.946 align:middle
So I don't know that
the demand will be quite

30:24.946 --> 30:29.116 align:middle
as strong as-as it
has been for the PPP.

30:29.116 --> 30:30.026 align:middle
I don't know that.

30:30.026 --> 30:34.156 align:middle
And the second thing is,
we won't run out of money.

30:34.336 --> 30:36.006 align:middle
You know, it's not
a limited pot,

30:36.006 --> 30:39.706 align:middle
so there won't be this incentive
to try to get there first

30:39.706 --> 30:40.446 align:middle
and that sort of thing.

30:40.446 --> 30:46.016 align:middle
So I'm-I'm hopeful we-we will
very much try to learn as much

30:46.016 --> 30:48.086 align:middle
as possible from that facility

30:48.086 --> 30:49.186 align:middle
and from all the
other ones, too.

30:49.186 --> 30:50.436 align:middle
We have a lot to learn here,

30:50.436 --> 30:53.106 align:middle
so we'll certainly
be trying to do that.

30:53.736 --> 30:58.956 align:middle
In terms of fiscal concern, so
I-you know, I'm-for many years,

30:58.956 --> 31:03.396 align:middle
I've been, before the Fed, I
have longtime been an advocate

31:03.396 --> 31:06.856 align:middle
for the need for the
United States to return

31:06.856 --> 31:08.776 align:middle
to a sustainable path

31:08.776 --> 31:10.786 align:middle
from a fiscal perspective
at the federal level.

31:11.206 --> 31:13.136 align:middle
We have not been on such
a path for some time,

31:13.136 --> 31:16.286 align:middle
which means-just means that
the debt is growing faster

31:16.286 --> 31:17.996 align:middle
than the economy.

31:18.246 --> 31:20.506 align:middle
This is not the time to
act on those concerns.

31:20.966 --> 31:23.516 align:middle
This is the time to use
the great fiscal power

31:23.516 --> 31:26.416 align:middle
of the United States
to-to do what we can

31:26.416 --> 31:28.906 align:middle
to support the economy and
try to get through this

31:28.906 --> 31:32.066 align:middle
with as little damage to the
longer-run productive capacity

31:32.066 --> 31:33.716 align:middle
of the economy as possible.

31:34.016 --> 31:37.186 align:middle
The time will come
again-and reasonably soon,

31:37.186 --> 31:41.416 align:middle
I think-where we can-where we
can think about a long-term way

31:41.716 --> 31:43.366 align:middle
to get our fiscal
house in order.

31:43.366 --> 31:44.956 align:middle
And we absolutely
need to do that.

31:45.526 --> 31:48.666 align:middle
But this is not the time
to be-in my personal view,

31:48.666 --> 31:52.016 align:middle
this is not the time
to-to let that concern,

31:52.126 --> 31:56.386 align:middle
which is a very serious concern,
but-to let that get in the way

31:56.386 --> 31:58.426 align:middle
of us winning this
battle, really.

31:58.486 --> 31:59.906 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

32:02.326 --> 32:03.086 align:middle
Okay, Edward Lawrence.

32:03.086 --> 32:03.716 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

32:03.716 --> 32:07.936 align:middle
Oh, yes. Thank you,
Mr. Chairman.

32:07.936 --> 32:09.416 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business Network.

32:09.416 --> 32:11.736 align:middle
Now, given the amount of
stimulus on the fiscal side

32:11.736 --> 32:14.416 align:middle
and monetary policy side,
how much weight do you give

32:14.416 --> 32:16.906 align:middle
to finding a vaccine,
from clearing

32:16.906 --> 32:18.896 align:middle
that uncertainty you talked
about around treatment

32:18.896 --> 32:22.296 align:middle
and whatnot, for the Federal
Reserve pulling back on some

32:22.296 --> 32:24.426 align:middle
of the Fed's actions
and raising the rates

32:24.426 --> 32:26.076 align:middle
from the zero lower bound?

32:26.076 --> 32:31.166 align:middle
Also, when does that Main Street
lending facility get deployed?

32:31.166 --> 32:33.166 align:middle
Because you talked
about "soon"-you know,

32:33.166 --> 32:35.416 align:middle
some businesses are in need now.

32:35.556 --> 32:36.246 align:middle
Thank you.

32:37.316 --> 32:38.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:38.116 --> 32:44.716 align:middle
You know, so we don't-we're not
in a position to make, you know,

32:44.946 --> 32:46.936 align:middle
a reliable assessment
of when a vaccine

32:46.936 --> 32:49.366 align:middle
or a therapeutic drug would be
ready, and so we're not going

32:49.366 --> 32:53.476 align:middle
to set our policies based
on-on our estimate of that.

32:54.126 --> 32:57.436 align:middle
We're going to just provide
the support that we can

32:57.436 --> 32:59.386 align:middle
with the tools that we have,
and we're going to keep doing

32:59.386 --> 33:02.096 align:middle
that until-until the
recovery's well under way.

33:02.936 --> 33:07.526 align:middle
In terms of Main Street,
so you know the story.

33:07.696 --> 33:11.226 align:middle
We put out a term sheet,
we got a lot of comments,

33:11.226 --> 33:13.226 align:middle
we took those very
much to heart.

33:13.726 --> 33:15.406 align:middle
We've spent a great
deal of time here.

33:15.686 --> 33:16.806 align:middle
It's a challenging space,

33:16.806 --> 33:18.606 align:middle
because it's many
different kinds

33:18.606 --> 33:20.766 align:middle
of borrowers-they
have different needs,

33:21.206 --> 33:22.696 align:middle
different sizes of companies.

33:22.696 --> 33:25.426 align:middle
And so, we're-as I
mentioned, we're very close

33:25.426 --> 33:27.816 align:middle
to announcing a new term sheet,

33:27.816 --> 33:29.736 align:middle
which will then become
operative fairly quickly.

33:30.036 --> 33:32.636 align:middle
My guess is, though, that
we'll keep-we'll keep looking

33:32.636 --> 33:34.856 align:middle
to add products and
add different kinds

33:34.856 --> 33:36.516 align:middle
of borrowers to that as we go.

33:36.516 --> 33:39.326 align:middle
And we're-we're well aware of
the importance of-of doing it

33:39.326 --> 33:40.396 align:middle
as quickly as possible.

33:41.176 --> 33:45.126 align:middle
We, here, you know,
we're very much

33:45.126 --> 33:47.126 align:middle
in touch with-with the
urgency of that need.

33:48.026 --> 33:48.726 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:50.076 --> 33:51.336 align:middle
Okay, Don Lee.

33:52.346 --> 33:55.916 align:middle
DON LEE. Chair Powell, it's
Don Lee from the L.A. Times.

33:55.916 --> 33:58.976 align:middle
I just wanted to follow up
on a question-the question

33:58.976 --> 33:59.986 align:middle
on the labor market.

34:00.586 --> 34:05.226 align:middle
I know you said that it's
highly uncertain but, you know,

34:05.226 --> 34:06.796 align:middle
there are analysts who think

34:06.796 --> 34:10.506 align:middle
that we'll have very high
unemployment until even the end

34:10.506 --> 34:12.976 align:middle
of next year, as
high as 9 percent.

34:12.976 --> 34:18.146 align:middle
And, at this point, what-can
you just talk a little bit more

34:18.146 --> 34:20.796 align:middle
about what you see as
the path of employment

34:20.796 --> 34:24.636 align:middle
in the coming months
and into next year?

34:24.636 --> 34:24.916 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:24.916 --> 34:29.096 align:middle
Sure. So unemployment's going
to go up to a high number

34:29.266 --> 34:33.136 align:middle
in the second quarter-uncertain
what the number will be.

34:33.986 --> 34:38.336 align:middle
When-and that's because so
much economic activity has been

34:38.336 --> 34:42.546 align:middle
shuttered, really, as we take
social-distancing measures.

34:43.486 --> 34:50.726 align:middle
And the-so sometime fairly soon
here, and probably gradually

34:50.726 --> 34:52.906 align:middle
and at different paces at
different parts of the country,

34:52.906 --> 34:55.796 align:middle
we'll see the social-distancing
measures rolled back.

34:55.796 --> 34:58.116 align:middle
People will begin
to spend more money.

34:58.116 --> 35:01.386 align:middle
It's, really, consumer spending
has fallen precipitously.

35:01.726 --> 35:04.536 align:middle
And once that starts to happen,
people will get hired back

35:04.536 --> 35:06.316 align:middle
and unemployment
will go back down.

35:06.716 --> 35:11.856 align:middle
I don't think it'll get anywhere
near the historically low levels

35:11.856 --> 35:15.166 align:middle
that we had in-as recently
as February, 3½ percent.

35:15.576 --> 35:17.766 align:middle
I think it will take some
time for that to happen,

35:17.766 --> 35:19.616 align:middle
for us to get back to anything

35:19.616 --> 35:21.316 align:middle
that resembles maximum
employment.

35:21.906 --> 35:23.796 align:middle
But, I mean, the main
thing we want is,

35:23.796 --> 35:25.016 align:middle
we want to get back
on that road.

35:25.016 --> 35:28.146 align:middle
We want to get that-that
recovery going

35:28.416 --> 35:30.926 align:middle
and get people back to
work as fast as we can.

35:31.676 --> 35:35.106 align:middle
Not faster than we can,
but as fast as we can.

35:35.296 --> 35:36.946 align:middle
The main thing is to
get into that stage

35:36.946 --> 35:39.606 align:middle
where the economy's healing,
where we have the disease

35:39.606 --> 35:42.396 align:middle
under control, where
we don't, you know,

35:42.396 --> 35:45.696 align:middle
take too much risk of-of second
and third waves and that sort

35:45.696 --> 35:47.726 align:middle
of thing, and get
people back to work.

35:47.726 --> 35:53.056 align:middle
And, you know, it's-again, the
path of it is highly uncertain,

35:53.736 --> 35:57.626 align:middle
but we will-we will be
there with our tools,

35:58.006 --> 36:00.256 align:middle
supporting the economy and
supporting that recovery.

36:00.256 --> 36:01.636 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:03.446 --> 36:04.866 align:middle
Scott Horsley.

36:04.866 --> 36:07.156 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

36:07.156 --> 36:08.086 align:middle
Thanks, Mr. Chairman.

36:08.086 --> 36:10.906 align:middle
After the financial crisis,
banks were instructed

36:10.906 --> 36:14.306 align:middle
to up their capital so they
could weather an economic shock.

36:14.756 --> 36:18.646 align:middle
What kind of steps do
you think we need to take

36:18.746 --> 36:22.736 align:middle
for the economy writ large
to make it more resilient

36:22.816 --> 36:23.846 align:middle
to this kind of shock?

36:23.846 --> 36:25.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:25.686 --> 36:26.226 align:middle
Can you just say

36:26.226 --> 36:29.396 align:middle
that part-you're a
little bit low volume.

36:29.396 --> 36:30.656 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

36:30.816 --> 36:33.916 align:middle
Yes. What kind of steps could
we take to help the economy

36:33.916 --> 36:38.166 align:middle
as a whole be more resilient to
this kind of exogenous shock?

36:38.346 --> 36:39.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:39.706 --> 36:44.416 align:middle
You know, it's an extraordinary,
extraordinary shock,

36:45.316 --> 36:47.836 align:middle
unlike anything, certainly,
that's happened in my lifetime.

36:48.616 --> 36:52.486 align:middle
And a couple things
come to mind.

36:52.486 --> 36:57.026 align:middle
I-I think the time will come
for a careful assessment

36:57.026 --> 36:58.446 align:middle
of the answers to
those questions.

36:58.896 --> 37:00.136 align:middle
It's early to be asking them.

37:00.136 --> 37:01.626 align:middle
We're still putting
out the fire.

37:01.626 --> 37:03.236 align:middle
We're still trying to win.

37:03.636 --> 37:05.346 align:middle
And I think we'll be
at that for a while.

37:05.346 --> 37:08.546 align:middle
You know, but I'd point
to a couple of directions.

37:08.546 --> 37:11.196 align:middle
You know, we worked hard
to strengthen the banks:

37:11.856 --> 37:15.996 align:middle
much higher levels of capital,
liquidity, far greater sense

37:15.996 --> 37:21.946 align:middle
of what the risks are they're
running and how to manage them.

37:21.946 --> 37:23.356 align:middle
So the breakdowns
that we've seen

37:23.356 --> 37:26.006 align:middle
in market function have really
been in the capital markets.

37:26.756 --> 37:31.246 align:middle
And I don't-I wouldn't rush
in with prudential regulation

37:31.246 --> 37:32.236 align:middle
into the capital markets.

37:32.276 --> 37:34.426 align:middle
But I think-and we did
plenty of things-you know,

37:34.426 --> 37:36.066 align:middle
triparty-you know, we
did a lot of reform

37:36.066 --> 37:38.226 align:middle
in the capital markets:
money market reform,

37:38.536 --> 37:40.946 align:middle
triparty repo reform,
central clearing,

37:41.356 --> 37:42.976 align:middle
all these important things.

37:42.976 --> 37:47.906 align:middle
But there-there will no doubt
be-with this-the size and force

37:47.906 --> 37:51.216 align:middle
of this shock will no
doubt reveal weaknesses

37:51.666 --> 37:55.426 align:middle
in the financial
architecture, and we'll have

37:55.426 --> 37:56.466 align:middle
to go to work on those.

37:57.136 --> 38:00.546 align:middle
I-I also think it-you know,

38:00.546 --> 38:03.776 align:middle
it tells you the importance
of-of getting your fiscal house

38:03.776 --> 38:04.176 align:middle
in order.

38:05.126 --> 38:11.216 align:middle
The U.S. really hadn't-really
hadn't gotten back

38:11.216 --> 38:14.996 align:middle
to where we needed to
get on fiscal policy.

38:15.546 --> 38:19.556 align:middle
And, you know, so we-we had
an already high level of debt

38:19.556 --> 38:23.696 align:middle
to GDP, and rising quickly,
when this shock arrived.

38:23.726 --> 38:26.876 align:middle
Now, we have the fiscal capacity
to deal with it, I believe.

38:27.646 --> 38:29.986 align:middle
But we will need
to-ideally, you would go

38:29.986 --> 38:33.056 align:middle
into an unexpected
shock like this

38:33.916 --> 38:36.966 align:middle
with a-with a much stronger
fiscal-fiscal posture.

38:36.966 --> 38:37.686 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:43.936 --> 38:44.736 align:middle
Mike McKee.

38:44.736 --> 38:48.576 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

38:48.576 --> 38:53.376 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, given the demand
drop, demand shock, and the drop

38:53.376 --> 38:57.426 align:middle
in oil prices, do you anticipate
that we might see any kind

38:57.426 --> 39:01.046 align:middle
of deflation, even for
a very short period,

39:01.046 --> 39:02.236 align:middle
that would require
a Fed response?

39:02.236 --> 39:06.546 align:middle
If we get a negative
print on CPI or PCE,

39:06.546 --> 39:09.666 align:middle
how should people
think about that?

39:09.866 --> 39:12.916 align:middle
And second question-there
is a disconnect, it appears,

39:12.916 --> 39:16.376 align:middle
between the markets and the
economic outlook right now.

39:16.376 --> 39:18.546 align:middle
And I know you said that
this isn't the time to worry

39:18.546 --> 39:22.826 align:middle
about moral hazard, but do you
worry, with the size of stimulus

39:22.826 --> 39:26.736 align:middle
that you and the Congress
are putting into the economy,

39:26.876 --> 39:29.366 align:middle
there could be financial
stability problems

39:29.486 --> 39:30.446 align:middle
as this goes along?

39:31.696 --> 39:32.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:32.046 --> 39:38.966 align:middle
In terms of inflation, we think
that inflation is very closely

39:38.966 --> 39:41.426 align:middle
and strongly related to
inflation expectations.

39:42.386 --> 39:45.746 align:middle
And during the Global Financial
Crisis, there was a concern

39:45.746 --> 39:48.826 align:middle
that we might see deflation,
but it didn't happen.

39:48.876 --> 39:51.076 align:middle
Inflation expect-inflation
tended to move

39:51.076 --> 39:53.946 align:middle
down a little bit, as it
will when demand is weak.

39:54.586 --> 39:57.776 align:middle
And-but inflation expectations
did not move strongly

39:57.886 --> 39:59.316 align:middle
down here in the United States.

39:59.696 --> 40:01.676 align:middle
They have in other places
in the world, though,

40:01.676 --> 40:03.066 align:middle
over the past 25 years.

40:03.666 --> 40:06.416 align:middle
There's been downward
pressure on inflation, really,

40:06.506 --> 40:07.966 align:middle
for several decades now.

40:08.456 --> 40:10.016 align:middle
So, I would say, as long

40:10.016 --> 40:12.216 align:middle
as inflation expectations
remain anchored,

40:12.846 --> 40:14.546 align:middle
then we shouldn't see deflation.

40:14.756 --> 40:17.116 align:middle
And the Federal Reserve
is strongly committed

40:17.116 --> 40:20.056 align:middle
to maintaining 2 percent
inflation over time.

40:20.706 --> 40:22.426 align:middle
So we'll be there
to work on that.

40:22.426 --> 40:25.066 align:middle
I think you asked, really,
about headline inflation.

40:25.416 --> 40:29.136 align:middle
If-if low energy prices-very
low energy prices were to-were

40:29.716 --> 40:35.856 align:middle
to drop headline inflation
negative, I-I would hope

40:35.856 --> 40:38.756 align:middle
that people would see through
that-and we'll be monitoring it

40:38.756 --> 40:41.576 align:middle
very carefully-would see through
it, though, and look to core,

40:41.576 --> 40:44.026 align:middle
which is a better predictor
of future inflation.

40:44.626 --> 40:47.336 align:middle
So, needless to say, we'll
be keeping very close track

40:47.336 --> 40:47.666 align:middle
of that.

40:48.456 --> 40:50.086 align:middle
In terms of the markets,
you know,

40:50.086 --> 40:53.566 align:middle
our-our concern is
that they be working.

40:54.356 --> 40:57.576 align:middle
We're not-we're not focused
on the level of asset prices

40:57.576 --> 41:01.476 align:middle
in particular, it's just-markets
are trying to price in something

41:01.626 --> 41:07.156 align:middle
that is so uncertain as to be
unknowable, which is the path

41:07.156 --> 41:11.126 align:middle
of this virus globally and
its effect on the economy.

41:11.976 --> 41:13.276 align:middle
And that's very,
very hard to do.

41:13.276 --> 41:16.016 align:middle
That's why you see
volatility the way it's been,

41:16.516 --> 41:19.906 align:middle
market reacting to-to things
with a lot of volatility.

41:20.446 --> 41:23.516 align:middle
But, you know, what we're
trying to assure, really,

41:23.616 --> 41:26.036 align:middle
is that the market is working.

41:26.326 --> 41:29.046 align:middle
The market is assessing
risks, lenders are lending,

41:29.046 --> 41:31.656 align:middle
borrowers are borrowing,
asset prices are moving

41:31.656 --> 41:32.746 align:middle
in response to events.

41:32.746 --> 41:35.936 align:middle
That's-that is really
important for everybody,

41:35.936 --> 41:38.956 align:middle
including-including, you know,

41:38.956 --> 41:40.596 align:middle
the-the most vulnerable
among us,

41:40.636 --> 41:42.966 align:middle
because if-if markets
stop working

41:42.966 --> 41:46.696 align:middle
and credit stops flowing, then
you see-that's when you see,

41:46.696 --> 41:50.736 align:middle
you know, very sharp
negative-even more negative

41:50.916 --> 41:51.856 align:middle
economic outcomes.

41:51.856 --> 41:55.306 align:middle
So I think our measures
have-have supported market

41:55.306 --> 41:56.576 align:middle
function pretty well.

41:56.576 --> 42:00.766 align:middle
You know, we're going
to stay very careful,

42:01.146 --> 42:02.606 align:middle
carefully monitoring that.

42:02.606 --> 42:05.346 align:middle
But I think it's been good
to see markets working again.

42:06.426 --> 42:07.756 align:middle
Particularly, the flow of credit

42:07.986 --> 42:10.876 align:middle
in the economy has
been a positive thing,

42:10.876 --> 42:13.526 align:middle
as businesses have been able to
build up their liquidity buffers

42:13.526 --> 42:16.746 align:middle
and households have been
able to be home-you know,

42:16.746 --> 42:19.326 align:middle
people have been home, you know,
concerned about their jobs,

42:19.326 --> 42:21.846 align:middle
but they're not concerned about
the financial system collapsing

42:21.846 --> 42:24.066 align:middle
as they were in 2008 and '09.

42:24.066 --> 42:24.726 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:26.986 --> 42:28.066 align:middle
Okay, Chris from the AP.

42:28.066 --> 42:30.406 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

42:30.406 --> 42:31.276 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

42:31.276 --> 42:34.076 align:middle
Chris Rugaber over
at AP, thank you.

42:34.076 --> 42:35.316 align:middle
I guess I had two questions.

42:35.316 --> 42:39.146 align:middle
I wanted to start on the
unemployment picture and nail

42:39.146 --> 42:41.826 align:middle
down a little bit how you
see-how you see things going

42:41.826 --> 42:42.406 align:middle
from here.

42:43.046 --> 42:46.986 align:middle
You did talk about potential
loss of skills over time.

42:46.986 --> 42:50.016 align:middle
So are you worried
about structural changes

42:50.016 --> 42:53.726 align:middle
in job markets that would
keep unemployment high and,

42:54.106 --> 42:58.746 align:middle
therefore, potentially
beyond the ability of the Fed

42:58.746 --> 43:01.986 align:middle
to do anything about, which
is something that was debated,

43:01.986 --> 43:05.306 align:middle
as you know, after the last
recession, and then eventually,

43:05.306 --> 43:06.926 align:middle
of course, the unemployment
rate did go lower

43:06.926 --> 43:07.856 align:middle
than people thought?

43:07.856 --> 43:10.596 align:middle
Second question is
just on the money

43:10.596 --> 43:12.906 align:middle
from Treasury, the $454 billion.

43:12.906 --> 43:17.206 align:middle
It sounds like you want to keep
that in reserve for programs

43:17.206 --> 43:19.476 align:middle
that have high demand, such
as the Main Street program.

43:19.476 --> 43:22.816 align:middle
Are you willing to use
that to backstop, say,

43:22.816 --> 43:24.796 align:middle
a program that is
having more losses?

43:25.026 --> 43:26.366 align:middle
I mean, you know,
what's your tolerance

43:26.366 --> 43:32.396 align:middle
for loss among that
$454 billion?

43:33.756 --> 43:34.496 align:middle
Thank you.

43:34.496 --> 43:34.906 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:34.966 --> 43:40.086 align:middle
So, in terms of the
labor market,

43:40.086 --> 43:43.676 align:middle
the-the risk to-of
damage to people's skills

43:43.676 --> 43:46.236 align:middle
and their careers and
their lives is a function

43:46.236 --> 43:47.466 align:middle
of time to some extent.

43:47.966 --> 43:52.986 align:middle
So the longer one is
unemployed, the harder it gets,

43:52.986 --> 43:55.016 align:middle
I think-and we've
probably all seen this

43:55.166 --> 43:58.756 align:middle
in our lives-the harder it is
to get back into the workforce

43:59.356 --> 44:00.596 align:middle
and get back to where you were,

44:00.596 --> 44:02.166 align:middle
if you ever do get
back to where you were.

44:02.166 --> 44:08.006 align:middle
So, you know, longer and deeper
downturns have had-have left

44:08.006 --> 44:09.316 align:middle
more of a mark, generally,

44:09.996 --> 44:12.176 align:middle
in that dimension
with the labor force.

44:12.176 --> 44:14.336 align:middle
And so that's why-as
I mentioned,

44:14.336 --> 44:16.986 align:middle
that's why the urgency in
doing what we can to prevent

44:16.986 --> 44:18.006 align:middle
that longer-run damage.

44:18.006 --> 44:19.186 align:middle
It doesn't have to be that way.

44:19.606 --> 44:21.206 align:middle
We won't be able
to limit all of it,

44:21.206 --> 44:25.506 align:middle
but we do have the tools to-to
do what we can to-to keep people

44:26.176 --> 44:28.106 align:middle
in touch with the labor
force and working and also

44:28.106 --> 44:29.266 align:middle
out of insolvency, too.

44:29.266 --> 44:34.826 align:middle
It doesn't seem fair that people
should lose everything they

44:34.826 --> 44:38.036 align:middle
have, including their
homes, over this.

44:38.036 --> 44:40.396 align:middle
So, nonetheless, there
will be some of that.

44:40.466 --> 44:42.606 align:middle
But we do have some
tools to ameliorate that.

44:43.456 --> 44:48.466 align:middle
In terms of the-so in terms
of the-the money, you know,

44:48.466 --> 44:58.286 align:middle
the $454 billion,
it's a couple things.

44:58.286 --> 45:01.546 align:middle
First, the Treasury Secretary
really has-has authority

45:01.546 --> 45:02.656 align:middle
over that, right?

45:03.186 --> 45:06.286 align:middle
And it stands in
front of our losses.

45:06.906 --> 45:15.486 align:middle
So, you know-but I do think
we are-we're clearly moving

45:15.486 --> 45:17.506 align:middle
into areas where
there is more risk

45:17.596 --> 45:18.856 align:middle
than there has been in the past.

45:19.636 --> 45:20.306 align:middle
And that's okay.

45:20.416 --> 45:21.786 align:middle
I think that's what
we're supposed to do.

45:21.786 --> 45:24.406 align:middle
This is-this is a
very unusual time.

45:25.226 --> 45:28.376 align:middle
And so-but in terms of
the-of the way to think

45:28.376 --> 45:30.276 align:middle
about that money, I think
that's really a question

45:30.276 --> 45:31.266 align:middle
for the Treasury Department.

45:31.266 --> 45:35.786 align:middle
You know, we are-we set up the
facilities, and we work very,

45:35.786 --> 45:38.996 align:middle
very closely and
successfully and collaboratively

45:38.996 --> 45:43.146 align:middle
with the Treasury on this, but
that-that particular aspect

45:43.146 --> 45:45.116 align:middle
of it falls more
to the Secretary.

45:46.096 --> 45:48.116 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

45:48.116 --> 45:49.886 align:middle
Okay, we'll go to
Nancy Marshall-Genzer

45:49.886 --> 45:50.746 align:middle
for the last question.

45:50.746 --> 45:52.706 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

45:52.706 --> 45:54.816 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

45:55.106 --> 45:58.246 align:middle
Chair Powell, I'm wondering
what you would say to savers

45:58.306 --> 46:02.146 align:middle
who are hurt by very low
interest rates and maybe going

46:02.146 --> 46:04.126 align:middle
into investments maybe
in the stock market

46:04.126 --> 46:05.216 align:middle
that aren't so great for them.

46:05.216 --> 46:08.846 align:middle
And also, I'm wondering if you
can give us any more of a clue

46:08.846 --> 46:10.766 align:middle
as to how long you
think we're going

46:10.766 --> 46:12.306 align:middle
to have interest
rates near zero.

46:13.956 --> 46:14.646 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:15.386 --> 46:22.546 align:middle
So we think that low interest
rates affect the economy

46:23.266 --> 46:26.426 align:middle
through a number of
channels in a positive way.

46:26.536 --> 46:28.676 align:middle
Lower interest rates
support economic activity

46:28.676 --> 46:31.486 align:middle
through channels that
we-overall, through channels

46:31.486 --> 46:33.666 align:middle
that we understand
reasonably well.

46:33.666 --> 46:36.986 align:middle
They make it cheaper to borrow,
they make-they drive, you know,

46:36.986 --> 46:38.466 align:middle
your costs of borrowing down.

46:38.776 --> 46:40.966 align:middle
They do-they do raise
asset prices,

46:40.966 --> 46:42.276 align:middle
including the value
of your home.

46:42.276 --> 46:44.866 align:middle
If your saver owns a
home or has a 401(k),

46:45.186 --> 46:48.376 align:middle
your saver will benefit
from that.

46:48.376 --> 46:52.336 align:middle
But for people who are
really, really just relying

46:52.336 --> 46:57.026 align:middle
on their bank savings account
earnings, this is-you know,

46:57.026 --> 47:00.096 align:middle
that's-that's-you're
not going to benefit

47:00.216 --> 47:01.406 align:middle
from low interest rates.

47:01.916 --> 47:04.746 align:middle
But we-you know, we have to look
out for the overall economy.

47:05.266 --> 47:06.906 align:middle
Low interest rates
support employment,

47:06.906 --> 47:09.796 align:middle
they support economic activity,
and those are our mandates.

47:09.796 --> 47:11.706 align:middle
And I think for the
overall good of the economy,

47:11.906 --> 47:14.266 align:middle
low interest rates
are a good thing.

47:14.876 --> 47:18.266 align:middle
And that's not to say they're
good for every single person,

47:18.456 --> 47:19.866 align:middle
but that shouldn't stop us

47:19.866 --> 47:22.976 align:middle
from doing what we think
is-is good for the whole.

47:23.666 --> 47:27.416 align:middle
In terms of how low, I really
don't want to speculate.

47:27.416 --> 47:30.516 align:middle
You know, we will turn to
questions like that soon enough.

47:30.556 --> 47:33.376 align:middle
But the-in terms of
how long we'll stay

47:33.376 --> 47:34.806 align:middle
and under what conditions
we'll stay

47:34.806 --> 47:36.006 align:middle
at the effective lower bound,

47:36.076 --> 47:39.026 align:middle
those are just exactly the
things we're thinking about.

47:39.026 --> 47:41.396 align:middle
Right now we like
the place we are.

47:41.396 --> 47:43.946 align:middle
We've said that we'll keep
our rates where they are

47:44.266 --> 47:46.406 align:middle
until we're confident that
the economy has weathered the

47:46.406 --> 47:48.886 align:middle
effects of the outbreak
and is on track

47:48.886 --> 47:50.656 align:middle
to achieve our-our goals.

47:51.066 --> 47:51.936 align:middle
So that's where we are.

47:51.936 --> 47:54.076 align:middle
We're leaving-we're not changing
that guidance today, you know,

47:54.616 --> 47:59.136 align:middle
but it'll be-that means we're
going to be very patient.

47:59.136 --> 48:00.886 align:middle
That means we're-we're
not going to be

48:00.886 --> 48:03.226 align:middle
in any hurry to move rates up.

48:04.766 --> 48:05.296 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

48:06.326 --> 48:07.216 align:middle
Thank you very much.

48:07.216 --> 48:07.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:07.526 --> 48:08.076 align:middle
Thanks.

